WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.491 --> 00:00:06.247
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.247 --> 00:00:09.570
You may be surprised that I'm wearing a
tie today.

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:15.500
Indeed bioinformaticians are hardly ever
dressed like this when they give talks.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:23.810
However 60 years ago, James Watson and
George Gamow form the RNA Tie Club.

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:28.570
The goal of this club was to decode the
genetic code.

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:31.422
There were 20 members in the club,

00:00:31.422 --> 00:00:34.918
one for each amino acid, and the mission
was

00:00:34.918 --> 00:00:40.570
to figure out which codons code these
particular amino acids.

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:46.400
The members of the club met twice a year,
smoked cigars, and drank wine.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:51.393
And moreover, they understood the
importance of mathematics and biology.

00:00:51.393 --> 00:00:53.343
So one of those 20 members was

00:00:53.343 --> 00:00:57.018
a famous mathematician, Nick Metropolis,
who was actually

00:00:57.018 --> 00:01:01.170
one of the founding fathers of
randomized algorithms.

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:07.030
However with all this firepower, there
were eight Nobel laureates in the club.

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:12.600
They have not decoded the genetic code.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:17.480
The honor of decoding genetic code went to
Marshall Neirenberg who did

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:22.140
it in 1961.
Today we will talk

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:30.020
about arguably the more difficult task,
decoding the known ribosomal code.

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:35.700
I'm sure that 99% of students in this
class, before the class started, thought

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:41.430
that the only way to produce, to assemble
amino acids into a protein or

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:48.170
a peptide, was to have a ribosome and to use
RNA as a template.

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:53.890
However, as we learned in the antibiotics
lesson, there is another highly

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:58.000
unusual mechanism for producing peptides
and

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:00.690
assembling amino acids into peptide
chains.

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:04.970
It's called non-ribosomal peptide
synthesis.

00:02:04.970 --> 00:02:14.340
And Mohamed Maraheil 15 years ago, set on
the goal to decode the non-ribosomal code.

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:19.082
Extremely difficult task.
Non-ribosomal peptide synthesis

00:02:19.082 --> 00:02:24.852
is performed by a giant protein called
non-ribosomal peptide synthetase

00:02:24.852 --> 00:02:28.940
or NRP synthetase.
And the task that

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:34.195
Marahiel was facing was this one.

00:02:34.195 --> 00:02:37.815
NRP synthetase in bacillus brevis, as we
learned

00:02:37.815 --> 00:02:41.820
code 10-amino acid peptide Tyrocydine.

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:45.270
But what particular amino acids in NRP
syntetase

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:50.535
are responsible for synthetizing, let's
say, valium in tyrocidine?

00:02:50.535 --> 00:02:55.815
Non-ribosomal synthetase in Streptomyces
Roseosporus

00:02:55.815 --> 00:03:01.800
codes for antibiotics of a last resort
called daptomycin.

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:05.050
But at the time when Maraheil started, it
was

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:10.310
absolutely unclear what particular segment
in NRP synthetase

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:15.480
in Streptomyces Roseosporus cause for
Daptomycin.

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:19.850
However, Maraheil knew something about
non-ribosomal peptide synthesis.

00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:21.550
It is performed, as I mentioned,

00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:26.350
by a giant molecular assembly line, NRP
synthetase.

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:30.260
And for every amino acid being
synthesized,

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:34.080
there is a segment in NRP syntetase

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:37.670
roughly 600 amino acids long called
adenylation domain.

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:44.660
So this adenylation domain synthesizes one
amino acid at a time, and since tyrocidine

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:50.810
is 10 amino acids long, there must be 10
adenylation domains in

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:56.730
NRP syntetase in this case.
So, what are the particular

00:03:56.730 --> 00:04:02.340
amino acids, or what is the non-ribosomal
code that decides what amino acid

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:07.415
to synthesize.
Well Maraheil started by

00:04:07.415 --> 00:04:12.810
taking a look at some adenalation demands,
and he argued that

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:18.550
despite, let's say, this three adenalation domains code
for different amino acid.

00:04:18.550 --> 00:04:22.300
So there must be something similar in
them, because they

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:25.520
all tried to accomplish the same goal,

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:28.562
to add one amino acid to the growing peptide
chain.

00:04:28.562 --> 00:04:31.292
He looked at the amino acids in these

00:04:31.292 --> 00:04:36.360
three adenalation domains and found very
little similarities.

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:38.965
There is hardly any similarity here

00:04:38.965 --> 00:04:43.330
only three conserved columns, which is
statistically insignificant.

00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:47.360
However, if you just start shifting some

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:52.480
of the sequences, like I just did, you see 11
conserved columns.

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:57.950
And if you continue doing this, suddenly
you reveal 19 conserved columns.

00:04:57.950 --> 00:05:01.650
This is statistically very significant,
which reveals

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:04.980
that these adenalation domains are indeed
similar.

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:10.450
We have just constructed the conserved
core of adenalation domain.

00:05:11.710 --> 00:05:12.640
The next line

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:17.040
of attack for Marahiel was to figure out

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:18.720
which positions in these adenalation
domains

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:22.380
are actually responsible for coding
different amino acids

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:24.340
or which of them are different?

00:05:25.390 --> 00:05:30.550
And he succeeded by finding eight blue
positions

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:35.960
that are actually responsible for
specificity of the amino acid synthesis.

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:37.200
As a result,

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:42.200
Marahiel was able to crack the non-ribosomal
code, and it turned out to

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:47.120
be extremely complex.
Eight amino acids located in different

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:53.750
parts of the adenalation domain, code a singe
amino acid as shown on this slide.

00:05:54.850 --> 00:05:57.560
But if we didn't move

00:05:57.560 --> 00:06:02.340
shift some amino acids in some
sequences compared

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:07.670
to others, we would never be able to find
the conserved blue columns.

00:06:07.670 --> 00:06:13.120
So it now looks like a card trick, a
magic trick that Marahiel perform.

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:17.970
How in the world he was able to figure out
how to align

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:22.064
sequences in such a way that he was able
to reveal the conserved column.

00:06:23.510 --> 00:06:28.700
Today, we will learn how biologists
compare biological

00:06:28.700 --> 00:06:34.150
sequences, but before we move there, I
will tell you about one more biological

00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:39.090
success story, the story of hunt for the
cystic fibrosis gene.

00:06:40.610 --> 00:06:44.510
We now move to 1980s, when biologists were
trying to

00:06:44.510 --> 00:06:49.120
find the cystic fibrosis gene.
Cystic fibrosis is an

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:53.710
often fatal disease of the respiratory
system that produces an

00:06:53.710 --> 00:06:57.340
excessive amount of mucus, a slimy
material

00:06:57.340 --> 00:07:01.170
that is secreted into fluids such as
saliva.

00:07:03.430 --> 00:07:07.970
Cystic fibrosis is a rather frequent
genetic disease.

00:07:07.970 --> 00:07:15.960
In the United States, one in 25 people
carries a faulty cystic fibrosis gene.

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:22.480
It doesn't mean that they have cystic
fibrosis, but if 2 people with faulty

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:28.570
cystic fibrosis genes have a child, there
is a 25% chance that the

00:07:28.570 --> 00:07:32.070
child will have cystic fibrosis.

00:07:32.070 --> 00:07:37.450
When in the late 1980s, biologists
narrowed the search

00:07:37.450 --> 00:07:41.520
for the cystic fibrosis genes to a small
region

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:46.260
on chromosome 7, the question arose which
particular gene

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:50.880
in this region is responsible for the
cystic fibrosis?

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:53.650
And at that time, genetic mapping
techniques were limited

00:07:53.650 --> 00:07:57.185
so they couldn't narrow the
search first.

00:07:59.780 --> 00:08:04.740
As a result, they started to look at those
genes, and decided to compare each

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:09.720
of the candidate genes in these narrow
regions to all known genes in that time.

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:14.920
And one of these comparisons revealed that

00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:20.380
one of those candidate genes in chromosome
7 is similar to an ATP binding protein

00:08:20.380 --> 00:08:26.650
that acts as transport
channel responsible for secretion.

00:08:27.330 --> 00:08:31.640
Remember, cystic fibrosis is related to
secretion, and

00:08:31.640 --> 00:08:35.860
ATP binding proteins are also related to
secretion.

00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:37.810
That was an eureka moment, and

00:08:37.810 --> 00:08:42.570
biologists conjectured that this specific
candidate gene,

00:08:42.570 --> 00:08:44.580
thanks to the similarity search,

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:48.159
is actually the gene responsible for cystic
fibrosis.

00:08:50.690 --> 00:08:56.160
They called the gene cystic fibrosistrans
membrane conductance regulator,

00:08:56.160 --> 00:09:01.340
or CFTR.
And the CFTR protein controls

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:06.009
the flow of ions in and out of cells
inside the lungs.

00:09:07.070 --> 00:09:11.900
If a mutation happens inside the CFTR
protein, then

00:09:11.900 --> 00:09:16.210
the flow of ions is disrupted, and it
leads to

00:09:16.210 --> 00:09:20.334
cystic fibrosis.
In fact, it turned out that just three

00:09:20.334 --> 00:09:26.740
nucleotide deletions in this gene disrupt
ion channels and causes cystic fibrosis.

00:09:29.074 --> 00:09:33.580
And now the time has come for us,
to look at what algorithms,

00:09:33.580 --> 00:09:38.644
biologists use to align
adenalation demands, or to

00:09:38.644 --> 00:09:45.412
compare various candidate genes
in the case of search for cystic fibrosis.

